   "content": [
      {
         "id": "prerequisites_section",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htChartMsg\"></span></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nWe recommend studying the previous texts in this section before continuing. They will help you understand the foundations we build upon here.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nHave you ever noticed how we create tables to organize information? Scientists do this with the scientific method, but so do we in everyday life—think of a bus schedule, a sports ranking, or a price list",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9a82e769-0cf6-4b17-8b98-63f27de3123e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Scientific method",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "Tables are useful because they let us look up results quickly. But here is the important part: a table only makes sense when we understand how it was built. In this text—and in the ones that follow—we will explore what multiplication tables really are and what they tell us, rather than just memorizing numbers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_why",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Why do we need a table?\n\nThe word \"multiply\" has a second meaning: \"to increase.\" This meaning connects directly to everything we have learned so far (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "8f3ff01f-f512-47ba-9a0d-cb3cac79cc3e",
                     "text": "The Britannica Dictionary",
                     "page": "multiply",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Family\n\nThink about a family. When a new child is born, we say the family \"is multiplying\"—meaning the number of people is growing. This is the idea of multiplication in its simplest form.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Slide 1</b>: Images of Altar Q at the archaeological site of Copán, Honduras. Photo taken during a visit on <htdate0>.<br />The names of the kings (<i>ajawob</i>) are detailed in the work of Anastasia Hutnick (<htcite0>).<br />The slide's source code was inspired by a template from <htcite1>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "85c2d88c-6ad8-4858-95f8-0f157c49bf07",
                     "text": "Hutnick, Anastasia",
                     "page": "pp. 18, 35 - 67.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2020",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cf7adc72-d340-412e-8d48-953ac64cb670",
                     "text": "W3Schools",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "-1",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "4",
                     "day": "26"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2020",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The altar is shaped like a big box, with inscriptions on top and sculpted human figures on its sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Forest\n\nAnother example: imagine a forest near Teotihuacán. A forest grows as more trees appear. Just like a family grows with new members, a forest grows with new trees.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgTeotihuacanGeneral\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgTeotihuacanGeneral', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: General view of Teotihuacán. Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "19"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "General view of Teotihuacan city. In this photo it is possible to observe different constructions in format \"Talud-Tablero\", and in the background we have pyramidal structures representing sacred moutains.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Simplifying\n\nLet's pause and think about these two examples:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Simplifying</b><br /><br />For families, we multiply people.<br />For forests, we multiply trees.<br /><br />Can we count them in the same way?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Great observation! In both cases—families and forests—we are counting living things. The counting method is the same, even though the objects are different. This is a key insight: multiplication works the same way regardless of what we are counting. With this idea in mind, we are ready to build our first results table.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_zero",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplication by Zero (0)\n\nWe start with zero (0), which represents the concept of emptiness or absence",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "576e4648-77fa-4a6d-9e51-9459ced964f3",
                     "text": "Musa, Auwalu",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b1a262fc-0148-489b-8b70-5266a85c1e67",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Numerical families (Part II)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Visualizing the Multiplication\n\nLet's try a simple experiment. Look at Table 1 and imagine we are counting people inside each numbered rectangle:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"ht-grid-container\"  style=\"background-image: url('images/Caral/CaralPiramideH1.jpg'); width: 100%; background-size: cover; background-position: center; background-repeat: no-repeat; margin: auto;\"><div>1</div><div>2</div><div>3</div><div>4</div><div>5</div><div>6</div><div>7</div><div>8</div><div>9</div><div>10</div><div>11</div><div>12</div><div>13</div><div>14</div><div>15</div><div>16</div><div>17</div><div>18</div><div>19</div><div>20</div></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Pyramid H1 at the archaeological site of Caral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate2>, during a site visit.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "19"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Multiplying by Zero</b><br />The numbered rectangles in the table represent groups used to sum the elements inside them. Suppose we are counting people within each rectangle.<br /><br />Upon observation, we conclude there are no people in any of the rectangles. Therefore, the total number of people in the first row is calculated as follows:<br />Total people = 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 4 x 0 = 0<br /><br />Expanding the count to the first two rows, the result remains the same:<br />Total people = 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 8  x 0 = 0</div></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This experiment shows us something powerful: no matter how many groups we count, if there is nothing in them, the total is always zero. It does not matter whether we are counting people, trees, or anything else—if the groups are empty, the result is zero.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: A Universal Rule</b><br /><br />What happens when any number is multiplied by zero?<br /><br />Think about it this way: can you find something in a place where nothing exists?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Building the Multiplication Table\n\nIn Practice 1, we used a formula to express our finding. Let's formalize it:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>0</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Formula used in Calculation 1 to represent repeated sums of zero elements.</p></div></div></center></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This formula tells us that when we multiply zero by any number N, the result is always zero. It is a simple rule, but a very powerful one.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "79d126c9-39ac-4679-904b-4ab69558e44e",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "- The **result** (left side) is the product.\n- The **factors** are on the right side.\n- **N** represents the group number in Table 1.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Applying Formula 1 to the first 10 groups in Table 1, we get the following results table:</p><p><div id=\"mParent0\"  style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Graphical Representation of the Table\n\nWe can also visualize these results on a graph. Let's think about what we expect to see:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: What does this suggest to you?</b><br /><br />When observing the values in the Multiplication Table of 0, what image comes to mind?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To graph these values, we use a number line—like the one we learned about before",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0713ca05-8889-427b-802e-f2d31f045c09",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking Among the Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"chart\"><div class=\"tick\"><span>0</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>1</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>2</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>3</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>4</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>5</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>6</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>7</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>8</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>9</span></div><div class=\"tick arrow rightarrow\"></div></div></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Numerical Axis. The numbers are arranged below a line, with an arrow at the end indicating the direction of increasing values. The CSS code used for this image is available on <htcite0>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c7edc93c-0a41-4753-8f89-9e52904ff003",
                     "text": "W3 Schools",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The line with an arrow in Chart 1 is called an axis. We will use two axes to plot our results:\n- The **vertical axis** (Product) shows the results.\n- The **horizontal axis** (Factors) shows the values of N.\n\nOur goal is to place a point for each N on the graph. Here is how it looks:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Points from the Multiplication Table of cero. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now let's connect the dots. Since every result is zero, all points sit on the horizontal line at height zero. When we connect them, we get a flat, straight line:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart2\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: Connected Points Forming a Straight Line. Chart created using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_one",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplication by One (1)\n\nNow that we understand multiplication by zero, let's explore what happens when we multiply by one (1). The same principles apply, but the results are very different.\n\n#### Understanding Multiplication by One\n\nA simple way to understand multiplication by one is to count on your fingers. Use the arrows in Table 2 to see this in action:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tr0\"><td><img id=\"lefthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('lefthand', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"righthand\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('righthand', '0%')\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\"style=\"font-size:4em;color:#00f;\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Examples of counting and multiplication by 1.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Multiplication Table Explained\n\nMultiplying a number by one means adding one repeatedly until you reach that number. For example, 3 × 1 means: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. The result is always the original number—except when that number is zero",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "We can express this rule with a simple formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Result of multiplication by one.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
